      <h2>The duopoly and the lithium transition</h2>
      <p>
        The two incumbents own the replacement market through nationwide distribution — the moat of the lead-acid
        era. The lithium transition tests that moat: the <strong>PLI scheme</strong> subsidises domestic cell
        manufacturing, and both incumbents plus new entrants are building cell plants, betting that the same
        distribution network can carry the next chemistry (see the
        <a href="https://market.worldbatteryhub.com/india-battery-market/">India market report</a>).
      </p>
      <h2>The duopoly numbers</h2>
      <p>
        The two incumbents together control the <strong>majority of India's organized lead-acid market</strong> —
        a duopoly built on dealer networks and brand trust that the lithium transition must now either convert
        or circumvent. The distribution moat, not the chemistry, is the contested asset (see
        <a href="https://market.worldbatteryhub.com/india-battery-market/">the India market report</a>).
      </p>


      <h2>Our Read</h2>
      <div class="box">
        <p><strong>My read:</strong> India's battery industry is the cleanest test of an incumbent question — can
        a distribution moat built on lead-acid carry a lithium transition? — and the PLI scheme is the policy
        forcing the answer.</p>
        <p><strong>Why:</strong> The duopoly's strength is reach, not chemistry. If the incumbents convert that reach
        into lithium sales, India's market keeps its structure; if the new entrants out-scale them, the duopoly
        era ends. Either way, the distribution network is the asset being contested.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>My editorial view, not investment advice.</em></p>
